BoosterTutor is a coach for Magic: the Gathering Limited. You draft, either against
a table of bots or by importing a draft from Arena, and it tells you which picks cost
you and why. Not a score out of ten. An actual explanation, the kind you'd get from
someone sitting next to you. It's live, it has accounts and real users' data to be
careful with, and I've built every piece of it myself: the data pipeline, the
backend, the front end, the deployment, and the prompts.

## The one rule

The whole thing rests on a rule I wrote into `CONTEXT.md` on day one: Python computes
every fact and every verdict, and the LLM only explains them. Ask a language model to
grade a pick and it will give you a confident number it can't back up. Hand it the
number and ask it to explain, and it stays useful. Everything else in the project is
shaped by that split.

## The data and the math

Card values come from [17Lands](https://www.17lands.com)' public datasets, loaded by
ingest jobs that run on a schedule through GitHub Actions. I measured those numbers
before trusting them, set by set, rather than borrowing from one format to fill in
another.

- **Fitness scores.** Every card gets a score for what it's worth to your specific pool
  at that moment, in percentage points of win rate, with terms for synergy and for how
  committed you are to a color pair.
- **Grading by gap.** Picks are graded on the distance to the best card in the pack,
  not on rank, with fixed bands and an `indifferent` verdict when nothing was at stake.
- **Archetype hypotheses.** Ten two-color archetypes get scored against your pool as
  competing guesses at what you're trying to become.
- **Deck ratings.** Letter grades for curve, mana, synergy and the rest, ranked against
  real 40-card decks, with an overall grade from a regression fitted per format. Mana
  is checked against Frank Karsten's published tables.
- **Backtests.** When I wanted a wheel-based "openness" signal, I tested it against
  tens of thousands of real picks, and the data told me to cut it down. Several design
  choices here exist because a number said so.

## Where the LLM comes in

Claude Haiku writes a note for each pick that deserves one, and Claude Sonnet writes a
longer summary of the whole draft. Both get a brief assembled from Python's verdicts,
so there's nothing for them to invent. The notes come from a wizard character named
Gnarl Edgibel, and they unlock with a daily allowance of Tomes.

- **Prompt and brief design.** The model sees structured facts and writes prose, and I
  designed that interface on paper before building any of it.
- **Cost controls.** Every call is logged with its cost, there's a hard daily spending
  cap, and per-user rate limits sit on every endpoint that spends money.
- **Honest scope.** A draft with nothing worth noting gets no note, because padding
  would only add noise.

## Practice drafts

You sit at a table with seven bots, open real packs from public draft data, and draft
all 42 picks. The bots are calibrated against about 1.5 million human picks so they
make believable mistakes, and I wrote down where they still don't match human tables
instead of pretending they do.

Each draft is pinned to a frozen snapshot of the format's stats, so verdicts never
shift under you, and any draft can be replayed from pick one as a Redraft that
branches into a tree. Before launching it I load-tested the live server: grading a pick
takes about 25 milliseconds of CPU.

## Privacy and platform rules

This is the part I'd point a hiring manager at. 17Lands asked me to stop fetching
drafts from their site, so I deleted that feature outright, along with the cached data.
The replacement reads Arena's `Player.log` locally in the browser, extracts only card
IDs and pick numbers, shows you exactly what will be sent, and sends nothing else,
because the raw file also holds account IDs and auth tokens. The parser is tested
against scrubbed logs. I also read Wizards' Fan Content Policy and kept the Tomes free
instead of selling them.

## The engineering around it

- **Backend.** FastAPI and SQLAlchemy on Postgres, with Alembic migrations, `uv` for
  packaging, and strict mypy, ruff and pytest on every change.
- **Front end.** SvelteKit, TypeScript and Tailwind, tested with Vitest in a real
  browser through Playwright.
- **Accounts.** Sign-in with email or Google, saved Studies, and per-user limits.
- **Deployment.** API on Render, web on Vercel, CI on GitHub Actions, with health
  endpoints and a Postgres-backed rate limiter.
- **Decision records.** Dozens of ADRs track how the scoring and architecture changed,
  including a full rewrite of the classification system after I realized one label
  was answering two different questions.

I've written about how it came together as I went: start with
